<aside class="electrical-safety-note" aria-label="Electrical safety and licensing">
  <p>
    <strong>Electrical safety and licensing:</strong>{" "}
    <a
      href="https://www.fire.nsw.gov.au/fire-safety/home-fire-safety/topics/electrical-power-boards"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
    >Fire and Rescue NSW's power-board guidance</a>{" "}
    identifies overloading, restricted ventilation, loose connections and damaged leads as safety
    risks. Electrical licensing is not administered as one national rule:{" "}
    <a
      href="https://www.erac.gov.au/about-erac/"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
    >the Electrical Regulatory Authorities Council explains</a>{" "}
    that Australian technical and safety regulation is largely a state and territory responsibility,
    and its{" "}
    <a
      href="https://www.erac.gov.au/licensing/electrical-licensing/"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
    >electrical licensing guidance</a>{" "}
    says certain electrical work requires the correct licence. Check the regulator for the
    jurisdiction where the work will occur.
  </p>
  <p>
    Treat new power points, fixed wiring, in-wall electrical alterations and installed electrical
    fittings as non-DIY wherever the local licensing rules require. For example,{" "}
    <a
      href="https://www.energysafe.vic.gov.au/DDIY"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
    >Energy Safe Victoria's Victorian guidance</a>{" "}
    includes changing power points and light switches as electrical work. This does not make ordinary
    furniture positioning or fitting a removable, non-electrical cable wrap licensed work. Do not
    alter plugs, leads or wiring, follow each product's instructions, and ask the relevant regulator
    or a licensed electrician when the boundary is unclear.
  </p>
</aside>

